Z||000000||My7Fieros||12-06-2001||02:00 AM||||Well I went to get in my car (86 V6 4 spd) and when I pushed the clutch pedal in, it kinda "popped". Now it only goes to maybe 3/4 of the way down and no further. Also there is no resistance in it when you push it. What happened? Now my poor baby is sitting in the Best Buy parking lot. Im headed down to The Fiero Factory in the morning (Thursday the 6th) w/a friend to pick up his Fiero, so I guess I'll talk to Ed about it. This <I>may</I> be a blessing though...Ive been wanting to convert to an automatic. Ive even got a good automatic tranny. Im gonna ask Ed about the difference in prices between fixing it and just swapping out.<BR>Anyway, any ideas on what could be wrong? I hate it when my baby breaks down!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> (Not to mention this is my daily driver!!!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> TIA<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-06-2001||03:23 AM||||Oh well its 2:15 in the morning and Im going to the Fiero Factory in a few hours. I'll just ask Ed. Thanx anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>(p.s. I <B>hate</B> manual trannies!)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eriamjh||12-06-2001||07:56 AM||||Sounds like the pressure plate fingers have broken.  The same happened to mine without the pop and the no-pressure feel. <P>When we yanked the trans out to change the clutch, there were broken fingers.  The plate wouldn't disengage the clutch when you pressed on the pedal.  It means you need a new clutch (or a new car).<P>I don't know about the automatic swap.  Sounds like a lot of work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||12-06-2001||10:06 AM||||I'd lean more towards a clutch fork giving out.  Mine sort of bound up a little, then popped.  Not only did I feel it in the peddle, but I heard it too.  It barely worked two more times so I could get into a parking lot.  By that time it was leaning on the pressure plate putting out a rattling grinding noise.  Needless to say, NONE of those components related to the problem are still in my car.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou_dias||12-06-2001||10:24 AM||||Could be the spring in the master-cylinder that pushes the pedal back up, it only comes up 3/4 of the way and little pedal pressure when it goes and the snapping of the spring may be the pop.||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||12-07-2001||03:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stsmithgt:<BR><B>I'd lean more towards a clutch fork giving out.  Mine sort of bound up a little, then popped.  Not only did I feel it in the peddle, but I heard it too.  It barely worked two more times so I could get into a parking lot.  By that time it was leaning on the pressure plate putting out a rattling grinding noise.  Needless to say, NONE of those components related to the problem are still in my car.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>We have a winner. According to Ed Parks (The Fiero Factory), the clutch fork broke. Dunno yet if it affected any other clutch parts. I'll prob just have a new clutch put in. <BR>BTW itll be around $950 to convert to an auto at the Fiero Factory. Prob just fix it now, and convert to an auto next summer.<P>||||reg||1
